Why the disclaimer wording is mandatory
The sentence “Theoretical scenario study. Not a forecast. Probabilities are conditional on assumptions listed in the Assumptions Register” is a content rule, enforced in three places so that it survives any single edit:

the footer and a fixed banner of every rendered page (05_website/_quarto.yml, disclaimer.html);
a disclaimer field inside 05_website/data/scenario_lookup.json, so the number and its framing travel together even in a raw data download;
methodology gate G6, which blocks a site build when a probability appears without it.

The reason is empirical, not aesthetic. In this design the probabilities are functions of the assumption set: with a single threshold moved from its low to its high bound, the probability of systemic instability moves by more than a third. A number separated from that sentence is not a compressed version of the finding; it is a different, false claim.

Falsification, per scenario
Each scenario file lists observable conditions that would show its mechanism is not operating - at least two each, named by indicator rather than by theme. A few examples, in the spirit of “the mechanism is contradicted”, not “the number fell”:

median real compensation growth statistically indistinguishable from productivity growth for four consecutive years, which removes B’s wedge mechanism;
the aggregate labour share stable or rising while AI capital expenditure rises, which is A’s precondition rather than B’s;
data-centre electricity growth exceeding efficiency growth for three consecutive years with capacity prices rising, which is C’s energy trigger, not A’s;
unrest levels returning to baseline after episodes in high-debt-service economies, which contradicts the ratchet that makes C a regime.
